  "result": "I wrote the test file at `tests/unit/intervals.test.ts` and ran it with `npx vitest run`. **11 tests pass and 4 fail.** All 4 failures come from a bug in `mergeIntervals`, not from mistakes in the tests.\n\n**The bug:** the doc comment says blocks that \"overlap or touch\" are merged, but `src/intervals.ts:17` checks `start < last[1]`. With strict `<`, blocks that only touch at an endpoint are never merged. For example, `[[1,2],[2,3]]` comes back as two blocks instead of `[[1,3]]`.\n\n**Failing tests (all involve touching blocks):**\n- merges intervals that touch at an endpoint\n- merges a chain of touching intervals\n- merges a zero-length interval touching another\n- produces sorted output with no touching or overlapping blocks\n\n**Passing tests cover:** empty input, a single block, separate blocks, overlapping blocks, a block inside another, unsorted input, zero-length blocks, duplicates, negative numbers, not changing the input, and returning new arrays rather than the input's.\n\nAs you asked, I didn't change `src/intervals.ts`. The fix would be to change `<` to `<=` on line 17. I left the failing tests as they are because they check what the doc comment promises. If touching blocks are actually supposed to stay separate, the doc comment is what's wrong, and I can change those four tests to match the current behaviour instead.",
